WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.402
Module 3 focused on retrieving, filtering, and changing individual rows.

00:00:05.402 --> 00:00:08.292
Module 4 changes the perspective.

00:00:08.292 --> 00:00:13.957
Instead of asking which records should I return, we often ask questions such as how many, how much,

00:00:13.957 --> 00:00:16.267
what is the average, or which group is largest.

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:21.707
A good aggregation starts with the business question.

00:00:21.707 --> 00:00:25.222
Then we define the output grain, choose the measures, apply row-level filters,

00:00:25.222 --> 00:00:29.597
apply group-level filters when needed, and finally validate the result.

00:00:29.597 --> 00:00:36.152
A simple way to think about it is detail, grain, measure, filter, and validate.

00:00:36.152 --> 00:00:40.432
If the final summary does not match the meaning of the question,

00:00:40.432 --> 00:00:45.482
even perfectly valid SQL can produce a misleading answer.

00:00:45.482 --> 00:00:49.077
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:49.077 --> 00:00:54.557
a retail report comparing revenue across products and regions.

00:00:54.557 --> 00:00:59.592
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:03.127
analyst can understand our work.

00:01:03.127 --> 00:01:09.847
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to

00:01:09.847 --> 00:01:13.412
defend later: what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:01:13.412 --> 00:01:17.467
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
